---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const meta = { title: 'Teaching — Upasana Sridhar', description: 'Courses, guest lectures, mentoring, and teaching development.' };
---
<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.description}>
  <section class="container section">
    <h1 class="section-heading">Teaching</h1>
    <p class="intro"> My teaching experience spans computer science introductions, high-performance computing and scientific computing. I strongly believe that good learning environments come from having a "just-hard-enough" challenge and an empowering classroom. You can read how I try to inculcate this environment in my teaching statement.</p>

    <h2>Courses taught and assisted</h2>
    <div class="teaching-list">
      <article class="teaching-entry">
        <div>
          <h3>How to Write Fast Code II</h3>
          <p class="teaching-role"><strong>Guest instructor</strong> · Spring 2024, Spring 2026</p>
          <p>Lectures on GPU architecture and programming and task-based parallelism in OpenMP.</p>
          <p class="teaching-role"><strong>Project mentor / subject-matter expert</strong> · Spring 2023, Spring 2024</p>
          <p>Provided questions and feedback during final presentations of student-led projects.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="teaching-date">2023–2026</p>
      </article>

      <article class="teaching-entry">
        <div>
          <h3>Computational Problem Solving for Engineers</h3>
          <p class="teaching-role"><strong>Teaching assistant</strong> · Spring 2025</p>
          <p>Designed recitations and assignment questions, ran office hours, and graded assignments.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="teaching-date">Spring 2025</p>
      </article>

      <article class="teaching-entry">
        <div>
          <h3>How to Write Fast Code I</h3>
          <p class="teaching-role"><strong>Guest instructor</strong> · Fall 2022, Fall 2023</p>
          <p>Delivered lectures introducing SIMD instructions and designing convolution kernels.</p>
          <p class="teaching-role"><strong>Teaching assistant</strong> · Fall 2020, Fall 2021</p>
          <p>Supervised nine teams of three to four students working on open-ended class projects. Ran office hours, graded homework, and managed a hybrid classroom.</p>
          <p class="teaching-role"><strong>Project mentor / subject-matter expert</strong> · Fall 2022, Fall 2023</p>
          <p>Provided feedback to project teams on their designs and final project presentations.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="teaching-date">2020–2023</p>
      </article>

      <article class="teaching-entry">
        <div>
          <h3>Advanced Algorithms</h3>
          <p class="teaching-role"><strong>Teaching assistant</strong> · Fall 2018</p>
          <p>Project-based course.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="teaching-date">Fall 2018</p>
      </article>

      <article class="teaching-entry">
        <div>
          <h3>Nature of Code</h3>
          <p class="teaching-role"><strong>Instructor</strong> · Jan.–Mar. 2018</p>
          <p>Student-taught course at PES University.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="teaching-date">Jan–Mar 2018</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2>Teaching training</h2>
    <ul class="training-list">
      <li>
        <div><strong>Teaching as Research (TAR) Institute, CMU Eberly Center</strong><p>A three-day program focused on developing skills to use data to inform teaching. Topics included data sources, study design, incorporating feedback, and presenting student outcomes.</p></div>
        <time>2022</time>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><strong>Evidence-Based Teaching in STEM, Carnegie Mellon University</strong><p>A semester-long course designed to prepare graduate students in STEM to teach effectively and efficiently as future faculty members; critically read and apply peer-reviewed STEM education research; and use approaches from the Scholarship of Teaching and Learning to assess student learning and iteratively improve teaching and course design.</p><p><strong>Course developed:</strong> <em>How to Think in Code—Introduction to CS for Non-Majors</em>.</p></div>
        <time>Spring 2024</time>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><strong>Future Faculty Program, CMU Eberly Center</strong><p>A program helping graduate students and postdoctoral scholars develop teaching skills and document their teaching-related professional development in preparation for a faculty career.</p></div>
        <time>Spring 2026</time>
      </li>
    </ul>
      <div class="teaching-influences">
      <h2>Teaching influences</h2>
      <p>A few writers and teachers have shaped how I think about teaching in CS.</p>
      <ul class="teaching-influences-list">
        <li>From <a href="https://archive.org/details/disciplineofprog0000dijk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Edsger Dijkstra’s <em>A Discipline of Programming</em></a>:- correctness—and, in my own work, performance—should be reasoned about through design rather than recovered after implementation.</li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/1118178.1118215" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jeannette Wing’s “Computational Thinking”</a>:- computing as the fundamental skill of formulating problems so that other entities, humans or machines, can participate in solving them.</li>
        <li>From <a href="https://archive.org/details/knittingwithoutt00zimm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Elizabeth Zimmermann’s <em>Knitting Without Tears</em></a>:- empowering learners with agency in their discipline. An education is developing an understanding of core principles, instead of learning to following instructions by rote. Her work revived knitting as a craft for an entire generation.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/pedagogy-of-the-oppressed-9781501314155/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paulo Freire’s <em>Pedagogy of the Oppressed</em></a>:- education is preparation to engage with a world that is “not as a static reality, but as a reality in process, in transformation.”</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.routledge.com/Teaching-to-Transgress-Education-as-the-Practice-of-Freedom/hooks/p/book/9780415908085" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">bell hooks’ <em>Teaching to Transgress</em></a>:- language for the classrooms in which I have learned best: intellectually demanding, participatory spaces where learning is something the class does together.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </section>
</BaseLayout>
